-- A+ Core 2 (220-1102) gap-fill expansion.
-- Targets six objectives, spread across three domains, that were missed by
-- earlier expansion passes and had little or no content:
--   1.10 Common features/tools of the macOS desktop OS
--   1.11 Common features/tools of the Linux client/desktop OS
--   2.9  Configuring a SOHO network
--   2.10 Browser security settings
--   4.8  Scripting basics for IT
--   4.9  Remote access technologies
-- Adds questions a2-q-1200..a2-q-1283 (84) and flashcards a2-f-1200..a2-f-1302 (103):
--   1.10 macOS ........................ 14 q + 17 f = 31
--   1.11 Linux ......................... 12 q + 14 f = 26
--   2.9  SOHO networking ............... 14 q + 17 f = 31
--   2.10 Browser security .............. 15 q + 18 f = 33
--   4.8  Scripting basics .............. 15 q + 18 f = 33
--   4.9  Remote access technologies .... 14 q + 19 f = 33
-- Domain totals: domain 1 = 26 q + 31 f = 57; domain 2 = 29 q + 35 f = 64;
--                domain 4 = 29 q + 37 f = 66. Grand total: 84 q + 103 f = 187.
-- Purely additive: no existing rows are modified or deleted.
-- Text uses dollar-quoting ($q$...$q$) throughout; no single-quoted string literals.

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 1.10 macOS (a2-q-1200..a2-q-1213)
($q$a2-q-1200$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which keyboard shortcut opens Spotlight search on a Mac?$q$,$q$["Command+Space","Control+Alt+Delete","Command+Option+Escape","Command+Tab"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Command+Space is the default keyboard shortcut that opens Spotlight, macOS's system-wide search tool for files, apps, and settings. Control+Alt+Delete is a Windows security-options concept with no macOS equivalent, Command+Option+Escape instead opens the Force Quit Applications window, and Command+Tab simply switches between already-open applications.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1201$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$A single application has frozen on a Mac and is not responding to any input. Which built-in tool lets a user close just that application without restarting the whole computer?$q$,$q$["Force Quit Applications (Command+Option+Escape)","Time Machine","Keychain Access","Disk Utility"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Force Quit Applications, opened with Command+Option+Escape (or from the Apple menu), lets a user select and quit a single unresponsive application without affecting anything else running or requiring a restart. Time Machine is a backup tool, Keychain Access manages saved credentials, and Disk Utility manages drives and volumes - none of them close a frozen application.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1202$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A technician is setting up Time Machine on a Mac for the first time. What does Time Machine require in order to begin creating backups?$q$,$q$["A separate external or network-attached backup destination with enough free space","An active internet connection to Apple's iCloud servers","A second Mac running the same macOS version","A USB flash drive formatted specifically as APFS-encrypted"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Time Machine backs up to a separate destination - an external drive, a dedicated internal volume, or a network destination such as a Time Capsule - and needs that destination connected with enough free space before its first backup can run. It does not require iCloud, a second Mac, or an encrypted drive specifically; encryption is optional, not mandatory.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1203$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$What is the primary purpose of Keychain Access on macOS?$q$,$q$["Securely storing and auto-filling saved passwords, Wi-Fi credentials, and certificates","Managing Time Machine backup schedules","Installing command-line packages","Repairing corrupted disk partitions"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Keychain Access is macOS's built-in credential manager, securely storing saved website passwords, Wi-Fi network passwords, certificates, and secure notes, and can auto-fill them when needed. It has no role in Time Machine scheduling, package installation, or disk partition repair.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1204$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which of the following statements about Homebrew on macOS are accurate? (Select two.)$q$,$q$["It is a command-line package manager for installing open-source software and command-line tools not bundled with macOS","Running brew commands as root/with sudo is the officially recommended way to use it","brew update refreshes Homebrew's list of available package versions, while brew upgrade installs newer versions of already-installed packages","It replaces the need for the built-in macOS Terminal application entirely"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Homebrew is a command-line package manager for installing open-source software and CLI tools not included with macOS, and its brew update command refreshes the local formula/cask index while brew upgrade installs newer versions of already-installed packages - two distinct steps. Running brew with sudo is explicitly discouraged since Homebrew manages user-owned directories, and Homebrew runs inside Terminal rather than replacing it.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1205$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$In Finder, what does pressing the spacebar on a selected file do?$q$,$q$["Opens a Quick Look preview of the file's contents without launching its full application","Permanently deletes the file","Renames the file","Compresses the file into a .zip archive"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Pressing the spacebar on a file selected in Finder opens a Quick Look preview, showing the file's contents (documents, images, PDFs, and more) without launching its full associated application. It does not delete, rename, or compress the file - those require different explicit commands.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1206$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A Mac's internal drive is reporting occasional file-system errors. Which built-in Disk Utility feature should a technician run first to check for and attempt to repair this kind of issue?$q$,$q$["First Aid","Time Machine snapshot browsing","Keychain Access repair","Spotlight re-index"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Disk Utility's First Aid feature checks a selected disk or volume for file-system errors and attempts to repair them non-destructively, making it the appropriate first step for reported file-system issues. Time Machine, Keychain Access, and Spotlight serve entirely different purposes and none of them diagnose or repair disk errors.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1207$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$ordering$q$,2,$q$Put these steps for restoring a single older version of a file using Time Machine into the correct order.$q$,$q$["Browse through the timeline/snapshots to find the desired point in time","Select the wanted version of the file and click Restore","Connect the Time Machine backup drive and launch the Time Machine app","Navigate to the folder that should contain the missing or older file"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$The correct sequence connects the Time Machine backup drive and opens the app, navigates to the folder that should contain the file, browses backward through the available snapshots to find the right point in time, and finally selects that version and restores it - skipping or reordering any of these steps leaves the technician unable to locate or recover the correct version.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1208$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$matching$q$,2,$q$Match each macOS tool to what it actually does.$q$,$q${"left":["Finder","Spotlight","Time Machine","Keychain Access"],"right":["Stores and auto-fills saved passwords and certificates","Creates versioned, incremental backups to an external destination","Browses files/folders and manages the file system","Performs system-wide search across files, apps, and settings"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,1,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$Finder browses the file system, Spotlight performs system-wide search, Time Machine creates versioned incremental backups to an external destination, and Keychain Access stores and auto-fills saved passwords and certificates - four distinct core macOS tools with non-overlapping jobs.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1209$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A technician opens Terminal on a Mac and wants to confirm which directory they are currently in before running further commands. Which command should they run?$q$,$q$["pwd","ls -l","chmod 755","sudo su"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$pwd (print working directory) outputs the full path of the directory the shell is currently in, which is the standard way to confirm your location before running further commands. ls -l lists directory contents rather than the current path, chmod changes permissions, and sudo su elevates to another user - none of them answer where you currently are.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1210$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$What happens when a user double-clicks a downloaded .dmg file on macOS?$q$,$q$["The disk image mounts as a virtual volume in Finder, from which its contents (often an app to drag into Applications) can be accessed","The file is immediately installed system-wide with no further action","It launches Keychain Access","It automatically creates a Time Machine backup"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Double-clicking a .dmg file mounts it as a virtual disk volume that appears in Finder, from which the user can access its contents - commonly an application to drag into the Applications folder. It does not install software automatically, and it has no direct connection to Keychain Access or Time Machine.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1211$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$multi$q$,3,$q$Which of the following statements correctly describe APFS versus the older Mac OS Extended (HFS+) format when reformatting a drive in Disk Utility? (Select two.)$q$,$q$["APFS is the modern default file system for Mac startup drives, optimized for SSDs and including native snapshot support","Mac OS Extended (HFS+) is required for a drive to be used as a Time Machine backup destination on all current macOS versions","APFS supports space-sharing among multiple volumes in the same container without pre-allocating fixed partition sizes","Only Mac OS Extended can be selected when erasing a drive in Disk Utility"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$APFS is Apple's modern default file system for Mac startup drives, built for SSD/flash storage with native snapshot support and the ability to share free space among multiple volumes in one container without pre-allocating fixed sizes. Current macOS versions support Time Machine backups to APFS-formatted destinations as well as HFS+, so that isn't an exclusive requirement, and Disk Utility offers a choice of formats when erasing a drive rather than being locked to just one.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1212$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A developer wants to install the open-source tool wget from the command line on a Mac that does not have it. Assuming Homebrew is already installed, which command accomplishes this?$q$,$q$["brew install wget","brew update wget","sudo brew install wget","apt install wget"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$brew install <formula> is the command that actually installs a given Homebrew package, such as wget. brew update only refreshes Homebrew's list of available formula/cask versions rather than installing anything, running brew with sudo is unnecessary and discouraged since Homebrew operates in user-owned directories, and apt is a Linux package manager with no macOS equivalent role here.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1213$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A technician needs to erase and reformat a USB flash drive in Disk Utility so it can be read and written by both a Mac and a Windows PC. Which format should be chosen?$q$,$q$["exFAT","APFS","Mac OS Extended (Journaled)","NTFS"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$exFAT is natively readable and writable by both current macOS and Windows without additional drivers, making it the standard choice for a drive that needs to move files between the two operating systems. APFS and Mac OS Extended are Mac-native formats Windows cannot read without third-party software, and NTFS is Windows-native - macOS can typically only read, not write to, NTFS volumes without extra software.$q$),
-- 1.11 Linux (a2-q-1214..a2-q-1225)
($q$a2-q-1214$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$In a Linux terminal, what does the ls -l command display for each file that plain ls does not?$q$,$q$["A long-format listing including permissions, owner, group, size, and modification date","A list of currently running processes","The contents of the /etc/passwd file","The system's total free disk space"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$ls -l produces a long-format listing that adds permissions, owner, group, file size, and modification date for each entry, information plain ls does not display. It has nothing to do with running processes, the contents of /etc/passwd, or disk space totals.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1215$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A shell script's permissions are set to 755 using chmod. What access does this actually grant?$q$,$q$["The owner can read, write, and execute; the group and everyone else can only read and execute","Only the owner can access the file in any way","Everyone, including the owner, can only read the file","The owner can read and write only; no one can execute it"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$In octal chmod notation, each digit represents read(4)+write(2)+execute(1) permissions for owner, group, and other respectively: 7 (owner) grants read+write+execute, and each 5 (group, other) grants read+execute only, matching the correct description exactly. The other options misstate who gets what level of access.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1216$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A file in a shared Linux project directory is currently owned by user alice, but user bob now needs to be its owner instead. Which command, run with appropriate privileges, changes that?$q$,$q$["chown bob filename","chmod bob filename","ls -l bob filename","passwd bob filename"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$chown changes a file's ownership; running chown bob filename (with sufficient privileges) reassigns the file's owner to bob. chmod changes permissions rather than ownership, ls -l only displays information without changing anything, and passwd changes a user account's password, not a file's owner.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1217$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Why do most Linux distributions and modern support best practices recommend using sudo for individual administrative commands instead of logging in directly as the root user?$q$,$q$["sudo lets a standard account run just the one needed command with elevated rights and logs which user did it, rather than granting an entire session unrestricted root access","sudo commands run slower than logging in as root, discouraging casual misuse","Root accounts cannot run graphical applications at all","sudo disables the ability to install any software"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$sudo lets an otherwise-standard account run one specific command with elevated privileges after authenticating with its own password, and that action is logged against the real user's identity - giving both a smaller blast radius per command and accountability that a full root login session lacks. sudo doesn't slow commands down, doesn't block GUI applications, and doesn't disable software installation.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1218$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A technician needs to install a package on a server and runs sudo dnf install httpd. Which family of Linux distributions is this server most likely running?$q$,$q$["A Red Hat-based distribution, such as Fedora or RHEL/CentOS","A Debian-based distribution, such as Ubuntu","macOS running Homebrew","A Windows Subsystem for Linux Ubuntu image using apt only"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$dnf (and its predecessor yum) is the package manager used on Red Hat-family distributions such as Fedora, RHEL, and CentOS, so a system running dnf install is most likely one of those. apt belongs to Debian-family distributions like Ubuntu, Homebrew is macOS-only, and WSL Ubuntu images use apt, not dnf.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1219$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which two Linux commands display information about currently running processes?$q$,$q$["ps","top","chmod","grep"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$ps prints a point-in-time snapshot of currently running processes, and top displays a continuously refreshing, interactive view of processes ranked by resource usage - both directly show process information. chmod changes file permissions and grep searches text content; neither lists processes.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1220$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A technician suspects a service has been repeatedly failing and wants to search a large log file at /var/log/syslog for every line containing the word 'error', without opening the file in a text editor. Which command does this?$q$,$q$["grep error /var/log/syslog","chmod error /var/log/syslog","ps error /var/log/syslog","cd error /var/log/syslog"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$grep <pattern> <file> searches the specified file's content for lines matching that pattern and prints the matches, exactly fitting the described need to find every error line in a log file. chmod, ps, and cd are unrelated to text searching - chmod changes permissions, ps lists processes, and cd changes the working directory.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1221$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$ordering$q$,2,$q$Put these steps for updating installed packages on a Debian-based Linux system into the correct order.$q$,$q$["Run sudo apt upgrade to install the newer versions of currently installed packages","Reboot the system if a new kernel or core library was updated","Run sudo apt update to refresh the local list of available package versions","Review the list of packages apt proposes to upgrade before confirming"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,0,3,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$The typical workflow is to refresh the package index first with sudo apt update, then run sudo apt upgrade, which lists the proposed package changes and waits for confirmation before proceeding, and finally reboot afterward only if a new kernel or core library was among the updates. Running the steps in a different order would either upgrade against a stale package list or reboot before the updates were even applied.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1222$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$matching$q$,2,$q$Match each Linux directory to its purpose.$q$,$q${"left":["/etc","/var/log","/home","/bin"],"right":["Holds log files generated by the system and services","Contains essential command binaries needed for basic system operation","Contains essential system-wide configuration files","Stores each regular user's personal files and settings"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,0,3,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$/etc holds essential system-wide and application configuration files, /var/log stores log files generated by the system and services, /home stores each regular user's personal files, and /bin contains the essential command binaries needed for basic system operation - four standard, distinct locations defined by the Linux Filesystem Hierarchy Standard.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1223$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$What does the pwd command print in a Linux terminal?$q$,$q$["The full path of the current working directory","The list of currently logged-in users","The system's IP address","The permissions of the current directory only"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$pwd (print working directory) prints the absolute path of the directory the shell session is currently positioned in. It does not list logged-in users, report an IP address, or show directory permissions - those require different commands (who, ip/ifconfig, and ls -l respectively).$q$),
($q$a2-q-1224$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A technician downloads a shell script named backup.sh and tries to run it with ./backup.sh, but the shell reports "Permission denied". What is the most likely fix?$q$,$q$["Add execute permission with chmod +x backup.sh, then run it again","Rename the file to remove the .sh extension","Move the file into the /etc directory","Run sudo reboot"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$A script needs its execute permission bit set before it can be run directly with ./scriptname; chmod +x backup.sh adds that missing execute permission for the appropriate user(s), after which running it again should succeed. Renaming the file, moving it to /etc, or rebooting the system do not affect the execute permission bit at all.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1225$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$multi$q$,3,$q$Which of the following statements accurately describe the difference between using sudo and logging in directly as root on Linux? (Select two.)$q$,$q$["Each sudo command is tied to the invoking user's account in system logs, aiding accountability","Logging in as root removes the safety net of being prompted per command, since the whole session already runs with full privileges","sudo can only be used to run graphical applications, never command-line tools","Only the root account is capable of installing software packages"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$sudo ties each elevated command back to the invoking user's own account in the system's logs, supporting accountability, and using it per-command means each action still requires typing sudo, unlike a root session where every subsequent command already runs with full privileges with no per-command friction. sudo works with graphical tools as well as command-line ones, and non-root accounts with sudo privileges can absolutely install packages.$q$),
-- 2.9 SOHO networking (a2-q-1226..a2-q-1239)
($q$a2-q-1226$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$When first configuring a new SOHO router, why should a technician change both the default administrator password and the default SSID?$q$,$q$["Default credentials and SSIDs are widely published and known, making an unchanged router an easy target for unauthorized access","Changing them is required before the router will connect to the internet at all","It increases the router's maximum wireless throughput","It is only a cosmetic change with no security benefit"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Default router administrator passwords (and often default SSIDs tied to a specific model) are widely published online, so leaving them unchanged means anyone who knows or looks up the defaults can log in and reconfigure the router. Changing them has no effect on whether the router connects to the internet and is a genuine security improvement, not merely cosmetic.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1227$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A user asks why their 5 GHz Wi-Fi network has noticeably shorter range through walls than the same router's 2.4 GHz network, despite often being faster up close. What explains this?$q$,$q$["Higher-frequency 5 GHz signals attenuate more quickly through walls and obstacles than lower-frequency 2.4 GHz signals, trading range for less interference and higher throughput","5 GHz is a wired-only standard and should not broadcast wirelessly at all","2.4 GHz always provides faster speeds than 5 GHz in every scenario","5 GHz requires a completely separate physical router"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Higher frequencies, like 5 GHz, carry more data but are absorbed and reflected more readily by walls and other obstacles, so they lose range and penetration compared to the lower-frequency 2.4 GHz band even though they often run faster up close with less interference. 5 GHz is a legitimate wireless standard requiring no separate router, and 2.4 GHz is not universally faster - it is generally the opposite in ideal conditions.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1228$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$In a crowded apartment building with many 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi networks, why do technicians commonly recommend manually setting a router to channel 1, 6, or 11 rather than leaving it on an arbitrary channel?$q$,$q$["Those three channels are spaced so they don't overlap with each other in the 2.4 GHz band, minimizing interference from neighboring networks on adjacent channels","Channels 1, 6, and 11 are the only channels legal to use indoors","Using any other channel disables WPA2 encryption","Those channels only work with 5 GHz-capable devices"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$In the 2.4 GHz band, channels 1, 6, and 11 (in most regulatory domains) are spaced widely enough apart that their signals don't overlap with each other, unlike adjacent channels which do overlap and cause mutual interference in crowded areas. There's no legal restriction limiting indoor use to just these channels, and channel choice has nothing to do with WPA2 encryption or 5 GHz compatibility.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1229$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A technician wants a security camera's NVR, sitting on the internal LAN, to be reachable from outside the network over the internet on a specific TCP port. Which SOHO router feature accomplishes this?$q$,$q$["Port forwarding, mapping an external port to the NVR's internal IP address and port","Enabling the guest network","Disabling the SSID broadcast","Lowering the wireless channel width"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Port forwarding maps a specific external port on the router to an internal device's IP address and port, letting inbound internet traffic reach that specific device rather than being blocked by the router's NAT/firewall by default. Enabling a guest network, disabling SSID broadcast, and adjusting channel width don't make an internal device reachable from outside the network.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1230$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which of the following are recommended best practices when configuring a new SOHO router? (Select three.)$q$,$q$["Change the default administrator password immediately","Disable WPS if it is not strictly needed","Keep the router's firmware up to date","Leave remote (WAN-side) administrative access enabled by default for convenience"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Changing default admin credentials, disabling WPS (whose PIN exchange is a known brute-force weakness), and keeping firmware updated to patch known vulnerabilities are all core SOHO router hardening steps. Leaving remote WAN-side administrative access enabled by default is the opposite of a best practice, since it exposes the router's management interface directly to the internet.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1231$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A homeowner has several smart-home IoT devices (a thermostat, a video doorbell, and smart plugs) with a history of receiving few security updates from their vendors. What SOHO network practice best limits the damage if one of these devices is compromised?$q$,$q$["Placing the IoT devices on a separate guest network or VLAN, isolated from computers and phones holding sensitive data","Assigning the IoT devices static public IP addresses so they are easier to track","Disabling the router's firewall so the IoT devices connect more reliably","Giving the IoT devices administrator access to the main router's settings"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Placing lower-security, infrequently updated IoT devices on a separate guest network or VLAN means that even if one of them is compromised, an attacker cannot use it as a stepping stone to reach computers or phones holding sensitive data on the main network. Assigning them public static IPs, disabling the firewall, or giving them admin access to the router would all make the situation worse, not better.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1232$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$What does an SSID identify on a wireless network?$q$,$q$["The human-readable name of a specific wireless network, shown to users choosing a network to join","The MAC address of the wireless router","The router's current firmware version","The encryption key used to join the network"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$An SSID is the human-readable name broadcast by a wireless access point that lets users identify and choose which Wi-Fi network to join. It is unrelated to the router's MAC address, its firmware version, or the encryption key used to actually join the network.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1233$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$An employee reports that an internal web application, reachable fine from inside the office, cannot be reached at all from a partner's office over the internet, even though port forwarding has been configured correctly. What SOHO router setting is the most likely remaining culprit?$q$,$q$["An inbound firewall rule is still blocking traffic on that port from external sources","The SSID broadcast is disabled","The wireless channel is set incorrectly","DHCP has run out of leases"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$If port forwarding is configured correctly but the service still can't be reached from outside, an inbound firewall rule on the router is the next most likely blocker, since NAT/firewall rules and port forwarding rules are separate layers that both need to allow the traffic. SSID broadcast status, wireless channel, and DHCP lease availability have no bearing on inbound internet connectivity to a wired or already-connected device.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1234$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$ordering$q$,2,$q$Put these steps for initially securing a brand-new SOHO router into the correct order.$q$,$q$["Enable WPA2/WPA3 encryption and set a strong Wi-Fi passphrase","Change the router's default administrator username/password","Update the router to the latest available firmware","Change the default SSID to something that doesn't reveal the router model or owner"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,2,3,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$A sensible hardening sequence starts by locking down the router's own management access (changing the default admin credentials), then applying the latest firmware to patch known vulnerabilities, then changing the default SSID so it doesn't reveal the router model or owner, and finally enabling strong Wi-Fi encryption with a strong passphrase to protect the wireless traffic itself.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1235$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$matching$q$,2,$q$Match each SOHO networking term to its description.$q$,$q${"left":["SSID","Port forwarding","DMZ host (SOHO router feature)","Guest network"],"right":["Exposes one internal device to all unsolicited inbound internet traffic, bypassing normal firewall filtering","The broadcast name identifying a specific wireless network","Provides visiting devices internet access while isolating them from the main internal network and its shared resources","Maps a specific external port to an internal device's IP address and port"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3,0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$An SSID is the broadcast name identifying a specific wireless network; port forwarding maps a specific external port to an internal device; a DMZ host exposes one internal device to essentially all unsolicited inbound traffic, bypassing normal firewall filtering; and a guest network gives visiting devices internet access while isolating them from the main network's shared resources - four distinct SOHO networking concepts.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1236$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A technician configures port forwarding to a network video recorder (NVR) using its current DHCP-assigned IP address. A few weeks later, remote access to the NVR stops working even though nothing was changed on the NVR itself. What most likely happened, and what is the best long-term fix?$q$,$q$["The DHCP server reassigned the NVR a different IP address; the fix is a DHCP reservation (or a static IP) so the address never changes","The router's SSID changed automatically; the fix is renaming the SSID back","The NVR's firmware auto-updated and disabled networking entirely","The wireless channel changed; the fix is switching back to the previous channel"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$A device configured with port forwarding pointing at a DHCP-assigned IP address will stop being reachable if the DHCP server later hands that address to a different device or reassigns it - the long-term fix is a DHCP reservation (or a manually configured static IP) that guarantees the device always keeps the same address the forwarding rule expects. SSID and wireless channel changes don't happen automatically and wouldn't explain this symptom, and firmware auto-updating doesn't disable networking outright.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1237$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$What is the main security concern with Wi-Fi Protected Setup (WPS) PIN mode on a SOHO router?$q$,$q$["Its 8-digit PIN can often be brute-forced in a relatively short time due to design weaknesses, potentially granting an attacker network access","It permanently disables the router's firewall","It only works with wired Ethernet connections","It broadcasts the admin password in plain text on the SSID name"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$WPS's 8-digit PIN authentication has a well-documented design flaw that lets attackers brute-force the PIN in a comparatively short amount of time, potentially gaining network access despite a strong Wi-Fi password. WPS has no effect on the firewall, works over wireless (not just wired) connections, and doesn't broadcast the admin password on the SSID.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1238$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$multi$q$,3,$q$Which two of the following should typically be disabled on a SOHO router to reduce its attack surface, unless there is a specific, deliberate reason to keep them on?$q$,$q$["Remote (WAN-side) administrative access to the router's management page","WPS (Wi-Fi Protected Setup)","Automatic firmware update checks","NAT (Network Address Translation)"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$WAN-side administrative access exposes the router's login page to the entire internet, and WPS's PIN weakness makes it a known attack vector - both are commonly recommended to be disabled unless a specific need requires them. Automatic firmware update checks help keep the router patched and should stay on, and NAT is a core function of a router's operation that must remain enabled, not something to disable for security.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1239$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$What is the primary purpose of enabling a separate guest network on a SOHO router?$q$,$q$["Letting visitors and untrusted devices access the internet while keeping them isolated from the main network's computers and shared files","Doubling the router's total wireless bandwidth","Automatically encrypting all traffic with a VPN","Allowing remote administrative access to the router from outside the building"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$A guest network provides visitors and other untrusted devices internet access while keeping them logically isolated from the main network's computers, shared folders, and printers, limiting what a compromised guest device could reach. It does not double bandwidth, automatically add VPN encryption, or enable remote administration - those are unrelated features.$q$),
-- 2.10 Browser security (a2-q-1240..a2-q-1254)
($q$a2-q-1240$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$What is the main purpose of a browser's built-in pop-up blocker?$q$,$q$["Preventing unrequested pop-up windows, often used for ads or scams, from automatically opening","Blocking all images from loading on a webpage","Preventing any webpage from using JavaScript at all","Automatically clearing browsing history every hour"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$A pop-up blocker is designed to stop unrequested windows - frequently used to display ads or run scams - from automatically opening as a page loads. It does not block images, disable JavaScript site-wide, or manage browsing history.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1241$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A user opens a private/incognito browsing window, assuming it will make them anonymous online. What is a correct limitation of private browsing mode?$q$,$q$["It only prevents the browser from saving local history, cookies, and form data for that session - it does not hide activity from the network operator, ISP, or visited websites","It routes all traffic through an anonymizing VPN automatically","It blocks all malware from being downloaded","It permanently deletes the user's saved passwords"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Private/incognito mode only changes what the local browser saves for that session - it avoids storing history, cookies, and form data locally - but it has no effect on what the visited websites, the network operator, or the ISP can observe about that traffic. It does not enable a VPN automatically, does not block malware, and does not delete previously saved passwords.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1242$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A user's browser displays a warning that a banking website's security certificate is invalid or does not match the domain name. What is the appropriate response?$q$,$q$["Do not proceed and verify the situation through another trusted channel before entering any credentials","Click through the warning immediately since certificate warnings are usually false positives","Clear the browser cache and the warning will resolve itself safely","Disable the browser's certificate checking permanently to avoid future warnings"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$When a certificate can't be verified as matching the site or hasn't expired properly, the safest response is to stop and confirm the situation through another trusted channel (such as calling the institution) before entering any sensitive information, since the warning could indicate a genuine attack in progress. Certificate warnings are not routine false positives to click through, clearing cache does not fix an actual certificate problem, and permanently disabling certificate checking removes a critical security protection entirely.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1243$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$What security benefit does a browser's process sandboxing (running each tab or site in its own isolated process) provide?$q$,$q$["It limits the damage a compromised or malicious tab/page can do, restricting its ability to access the rest of the system or other tabs","It prevents the browser from ever crashing","It automatically blocks all pop-up windows","It encrypts all data stored on the hard drive"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Sandboxing runs each tab, site, or plugin in its own isolated process, so a compromised or malicious page has a much harder time reaching the rest of the browser, the operating system, or other open tabs. It doesn't guarantee the browser never crashes, isn't what blocks pop-ups, and doesn't itself encrypt data stored on disk.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1244$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which of the following are legitimate concerns when a user is considering installing a new, unfamiliar browser extension? (Select two.)$q$,$q$["The extension may request broad permissions, such as reading and changing data on every website visited","An extension from an unverified source could inject ads, track browsing behavior, or exfiltrate data","All browser extensions are cryptographically verified as safe before being listed in any store","Extensions cannot access any webpage content under any circumstances"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Unfamiliar extensions can request broad permissions - such as reading and modifying data on every website visited - and, if from an unverified source, could use that access to inject ads, track behavior, or exfiltrate data. Browser stores apply only baseline review, not a guarantee of safety for every listed extension, and extensions with the right permissions absolutely can access webpage content.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1245$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$A user is stuck in a login loop on a website that used to work fine. Clearing the browser's cache and cookies for that site often resolves this because it:$q$,$q$["Removes corrupted or outdated stored site data and session cookies that may be causing the site to behave incorrectly","Permanently deletes the website from the internet","Disables the site's ability to ever set cookies again","Uninstalls any extensions related to that site"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Clearing cache and cookies removes locally stored, potentially corrupted or outdated site data and session cookies that can cause login loops or other odd behavior, letting the site start fresh. It does not delete the website itself, does not permanently prevent future cookies, and has no effect on installed extensions.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1246$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A user's browser has become sluggish and is frequently redirecting to unexpected advertising pages that were not clicked on. What should a technician check first?$q$,$q$["The list of installed browser extensions/add-ons for anything unfamiliar or recently added, and remove suspicious ones","The monitor's refresh rate settings","The wireless router's channel setting","The computer's BIOS/UEFI version"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Sudden ad redirects and sluggish performance are classic symptoms of a malicious or adware-bundled browser extension, so reviewing the installed extensions list for anything unfamiliar or recently added - and removing suspicious entries - is the appropriate first troubleshooting step. Monitor refresh rate, router wireless channel, and BIOS/UEFI version have no connection to browser-level ad injection.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1247$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$How does a password manager's autofill feature help protect against certain phishing attempts, beyond simply remembering passwords?$q$,$q$["It only autofills credentials on the exact legitimate domain they were saved for, so it typically will not autofill on a similar-looking but fraudulent look-alike domain","It automatically reports phishing sites to law enforcement","It prevents any website from ever asking for a password","It disables JavaScript on every site visited"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$A password manager ties each saved credential to the exact domain it was created for, so it generally will not offer to autofill on a similar-looking but different fraudulent domain the way a user typing manually might not notice. It does not report sites to law enforcement, does not prevent sites from requesting passwords, and has no effect on JavaScript.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1248$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$ordering$q$,2,$q$Put these general steps for clearing a browser's cached data and cookies through its settings menu into the correct order.$q$,$q$["Confirm the action to clear the selected data","Open the browser's settings/privacy menu","Choose a time range and select which data types to clear (cache, cookies, etc.)","Locate the clear browsing data option"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3,2,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$The typical flow opens the browser's settings/privacy menu, locates the specific clear browsing data option, chooses a time range and which data types (cache, cookies, etc.) to remove, and finally confirms the action - skipping ahead to confirming before selecting what to clear isn't possible in the actual interface.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1249$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$matching$q$,2,$q$Match each browser security feature to its description.$q$,$q${"left":["Pop-up blocker","Private/incognito mode","Certificate warning","Sandboxing"],"right":["Isolates a tab or site's process so a compromise or crash has limited effect on the rest of the browser/system","Alerts that a site's identity or encryption could not be properly verified","Suppresses unrequested windows that open automatically, often used for ads or scams","Avoids saving local history, cookies, and form data for that browsing session only"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,1,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$A pop-up blocker suppresses unrequested windows often used for ads or scams; private/incognito mode avoids saving local history, cookies, and form data for that session; a certificate warning alerts that a site's identity or encryption could not be verified; and sandboxing isolates a tab or site's process to limit the effect of a compromise or crash - four distinct browser security features.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1250$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$In the context of a web browser, what is an extension (also sometimes called an add-on)?$q$,$q$["A small, optional software module that adds extra features or functionality to the browser, such as an ad blocker or password manager toolbar","A required component without which the browser cannot start","A type of certificate used to verify website identity","A setting that controls only the browser's color theme"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$An extension (or add-on) is a small, optional software module that adds extra functionality to the browser beyond what it ships with by default, such as an ad blocker or password manager toolbar. It is not a required startup component, not a type of certificate, and not limited to color themes.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1251$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$While connecting to a coffee shop's public Wi-Fi, a user's browser suddenly shows a certificate warning for a normally trusted banking site they routinely visit without any issue. What is the safest response?$q$,$q$["Treat it as a red flag for a possible man-in-the-middle attack on the untrusted network, decline to proceed, and avoid entering credentials until on a trusted network","Proceed immediately, since public Wi-Fi networks are known to always cause harmless certificate warnings","Restart the browser and the certificate will automatically become valid","Switch to a different browser on the same network, which will bypass the warning"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$An unexpected certificate warning on a normally trusted site, especially right after joining an unfamiliar public network, is a classic sign of a possible man-in-the-middle interception attempting to present a fraudulent certificate; the safe response is to decline and avoid entering credentials until on a trusted network. Public Wi-Fi does not inherently and harmlessly cause certificate warnings, restarting the browser doesn't fix an actual certificate mismatch, and switching browsers on the same compromised network wouldn't resolve the underlying issue.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1252$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$multi$q$,3,$q$Which of the following are accurate statements about using a reputable password manager? (Select two.)$q$,$q$["It can generate long, unique, random passwords for every site, reducing the impact of any single site's data breach","It stores credentials in an encrypted vault protected by a master password/key","Using one guarantees complete immunity from every phishing attempt","It requires reusing the same password across every account for the manager to function"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$A reputable password manager can generate long, unique, random passwords per site (limiting the blast radius of any one breach) and stores them in an encrypted vault protected by a master key. It does not guarantee complete immunity from every phishing attempt - other safeguards like careful domain-checking and MFA still matter - and it works best precisely because it avoids password reuse, not because it requires it.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1253$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Why should a technician review the specific permissions an extension requests (such as "read and change all your data on all websites") before installing it, rather than approving every request by default?$q$,$q$["Granting only the access an extension genuinely needs limits what a compromised or poorly-behaved extension could do, following the principle of least privilege","Extension permissions are purely cosmetic and have no real effect","Browsers do not allow extensions to request any permissions at all","Reviewing permissions is only relevant for extensions downloaded outside the official store"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Reviewing exactly what permissions an extension requests, and only granting what its stated purpose actually needs, follows the principle of least privilege and limits the damage a compromised or poorly-behaved extension could do. Permissions have real functional effect, browsers do let extensions request specific permissions, and reviewing them matters regardless of whether the extension came from an official store or elsewhere.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1254$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which of the following is NOT retained locally on the computer after a private/incognito browsing window is closed?$q$,$q$["Browsing history, cookies, and form data entered during that private session","Files that were explicitly downloaded and saved to disk during the session","Bookmarks that were manually added during the session","None of the above - private mode leaves no trace of anything at all, including downloaded files"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Private/incognito mode's defining behavior is that it does not save browsing history, cookies, or form data locally for that session once the window closes. Files explicitly downloaded to disk and bookmarks manually added during the session are both still saved normally, since private mode only affects the automatically recorded browsing data, not deliberate user actions like saving a file or a bookmark.$q$),
-- 4.8 Scripting basics (a2-q-1255..a2-q-1269)
($q$a2-q-1255$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which line correctly assigns the value 5 to a variable in a Bash script?$q$,$q$["count=5","count = 5","$count = 5","let count == 5"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Bash variable assignment requires no spaces around the equals sign, so count=5 is correct syntax. Adding spaces around the =, prefixing the variable name with $ during assignment, or using a let statement with == are all incorrect for a basic assignment in Bash.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1256$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$In a basic scripting language, what does a for loop generally do?$q$,$q$["Repeats a block of commands once for each item in a defined list or range","Permanently pauses script execution until a key is pressed","Declares a new variable without assigning it a value","Immediately terminates the script if an error occurs"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$A for loop repeats a block of commands once for each item in a defined list or range, which is its defining behavior across scripting languages. It does not pause execution for keyboard input, does not just declare a variable, and does not terminate the script on error - those are unrelated behaviors.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1257$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$ordering$q$,2,$q$Arrange these lines into a working Bash loop that prints the numbers 1, 2, and 3, each on its own line.$q$,$q$["done","do","  echo $i","for i in 1 2 3;"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[3,1,2,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$A working Bash for loop is written as "for i in 1 2 3; do", followed by the loop body ("echo $i"), and closed with "done" - in that exact structural order. Rearranging any of these lines would produce a syntax error or fail to actually loop over the values.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1258$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Why is running an unreviewed script with administrator/root privileges considered risky?$q$,$q$["Any destructive or malicious command in the script executes with full system-level access, with no built-in check on what it is allowed to do","Elevated scripts always run more slowly than normal scripts","Scripts cannot access the file system when run with elevated privileges","Administrator privileges automatically scan the script for malware before running it"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$A script run with administrator/root privileges executes every command it contains with that same full level of access, so any destructive, buggy, or malicious command in an unreviewed script runs unchecked at the system level. Elevated privileges don't inherently slow scripts down, don't block file-system access, and running with elevated rights doesn't trigger any automatic malware scan.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1259$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which of the following are recognized risks in poorly written or unreviewed IT scripts? (Select two.)$q$,$q$["Hardcoded plaintext credentials embedded directly in the script","Missing input validation, allowing unexpected or malicious input to cause unintended actions","Including comments that explain what each section of the script does","Using consistent indentation for readability"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Hardcoded plaintext credentials expose sensitive login information to anyone who can read the script file, and missing input validation lets unexpected or malicious input trigger unintended actions - both are well-documented scripting risks. Comments and consistent indentation are best practices that improve readability and maintainability, not risks.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1260$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which of the following is a typical, appropriate use case for a simple IT automation script?$q$,$q$["Automatically renaming or organizing hundreds of files according to a consistent pattern","Manually clicking through each file one at a time in a file explorer window","Physically replacing a failed hard drive","Reading a printed user manual"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Automating a repetitive, well-defined task like renaming or organizing many files according to a consistent pattern is exactly the kind of work scripting is meant to speed up and make consistent. Manually clicking through files, physically replacing hardware, and reading a manual are unrelated to what a script accomplishes.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1261$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$Why have modern versions of Windows and Office increasingly restricted or deprecated VBScript execution by default?$q$,$q$["VBScript has historically been a common vector for malicious macros and scripts delivered through phishing emails and documents","VBScript scripts run significantly faster than PowerShell scripts, causing performance issues","VBScript is only capable of formatting text, not executing any commands","Microsoft never included VBScript support in any version of Windows"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$VBScript has a long history of being embedded in malicious documents and email attachments to deliver malware, which is why Microsoft has progressively restricted or disabled its automatic execution in modern Windows and Office by default. Its deprecation isn't due to speed, and VBScript can execute a wide range of commands beyond text formatting - it has been included in Windows for decades, not absent entirely.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1262$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$What is distinctive about how Python defines code blocks (such as the body of a loop or an if statement) compared to Bash or PowerShell?$q$,$q$["Python uses consistent indentation itself to define a block's boundaries, rather than keywords like done/fi or curly braces","Python requires every line to end with a semicolon or the script will not run","Python cannot contain loops of any kind","Python blocks must always be written on a single line"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Python uses consistent indentation itself to mark which lines belong inside a loop, function, or conditional block, rather than relying on closing keywords like done/fi (as in Bash) or curly braces (as in many other languages). Python does not require semicolons at the end of every line, does support loops extensively, and blocks are not restricted to a single line.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1263$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$matching$q$,2,$q$Match each scripting language to its standard file extension.$q$,$q${"left":["Windows batch script","PowerShell script","Python script","Bash shell script"],"right":[".sh",".py",".bat",".ps1"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,1,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$Windows batch scripts use the .bat extension, PowerShell scripts use .ps1, Python scripts use .py, and Bash shell scripts use .sh - the standard file extension conventions for each of these scripting environments.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1264$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$In the context of a script, what is a variable?$q$,$q$["A named storage location that holds a value which can be referenced and changed as the script runs","A line of text explaining what the script does, ignored when the script runs","A fixed value that can never change during execution","The file extension used to identify the scripting language"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$A variable is a named storage location in a script that holds a value which can be referenced and changed as the script executes, letting the same logic work with different data. It is not documentation text (that's a comment), not something that's inherently fixed forever, and not the same thing as a file extension.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1265$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$multi$q$,3,$q$Before running an unfamiliar script downloaded from the internet on a production machine, which of the following are recommended best practices? (Select three.)$q$,$q$["Review the script's source code to understand what commands it will actually run","Test it first in an isolated sandbox or virtual machine","Run it with only the level of privilege actually required for the task, not full administrator/root by default","Run it immediately as administrator/root without reading it, to save time"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Reviewing a script's actual commands, testing it first in an isolated sandbox or VM, and running it with only the privilege level the task genuinely requires are all standard precautions before trusting an unfamiliar script on a production system. Running it immediately as administrator/root without reading it first is the risky behavior these precautions exist specifically to prevent.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1266$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$What is the purpose of PowerShell's execution policy setting?$q$,$q$["It controls under what conditions PowerShell scripts are allowed to run on that system, such as requiring scripts to be digitally signed","It sets the maximum number of variables a script can declare","It determines which version of Windows PowerShell is installed","It automatically formats a script's indentation"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$PowerShell's execution policy controls the conditions under which scripts are permitted to run on a system, such as requiring that scripts be digitally signed before executing - a security control against running unauthorized or untrusted scripts. It has no relationship to the number of variables a script declares, which PowerShell version is installed, or automatic formatting.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1267$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A logon batch script already contains several net use lines mapping different drive letters, each on its own line, and each line runs independently in sequence. A technician needs to add one more mapped drive for a new share. Where should the new net use line be placed?$q$,$q$["Anywhere among the other net use lines, since each line executes independently and their order relative to each other does not matter","Only as the very first line of the entire script, or the script will fail to run","Only after every single other command in the script has already finished, including unrelated ones","It cannot be added without rewriting the entire script from scratch"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Because each net use line in this script is an independent command that runs in its own right regardless of the other mapping lines around it, a new mapping can be inserted anywhere among those similar lines without breaking the script's logic. There is no requirement that it be the very first line, that it come only after every unrelated command finishes, or that the entire script be rewritten just to add one more line.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1268$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$What is the core purpose of a loop in a script?$q$,$q$["To repeat a block of instructions multiple times, either a set number of times or until a condition is met","To permanently stop the script from running","To declare the script's file name","To display a graphical dialog box only"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$A loop's core purpose is to repeat a block of instructions - either a set number of times or until some condition is met - so the same logic doesn't have to be written out repeatedly by hand. It has nothing to do with permanently stopping a script, naming the script file, or restricting output to a graphical dialog.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1269$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$What generally distinguishes a scripting language like Bash, Python, or PowerShell from a compiled language like C, in terms of how code is run?$q$,$q$["Scripts are typically read and executed line by line by an interpreter at run time, without a separate compilation step producing a standalone binary beforehand","Scripting languages must always be converted into machine code and saved as a .exe before they can run at all","Compiled languages cannot be automated or scheduled to run unattended","Scripting languages can only run on Linux, never on Windows or macOS"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Scripting languages like Bash, Python, and PowerShell are typically interpreted line by line at run time by their respective interpreter, without a separate compilation step producing a standalone executable beforehand, unlike a compiled language such as C. Scripts don't need to be converted into a .exe first, compiled languages can absolutely be automated and scheduled, and scripting languages run across Windows, macOS, and Linux, not just Linux.$q$),
-- 4.9 Remote access technologies (a2-q-1270..a2-q-1283)
($q$a2-q-1270$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$What is the default TCP port used by Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP)?$q$,$q$["3389","22","5900","443"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$RDP uses TCP port 3389 by default for its remote graphical desktop sessions. Port 22 belongs to SSH, port 5900 belongs to VNC, and port 443 is used for standard HTTPS traffic - none of them are RDP's default port.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1271$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$What is the default TCP port used by SSH (Secure Shell)?$q$,$q$["22","3389","5900","80"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$SSH uses TCP port 22 by default for its encrypted remote command-line sessions. Port 3389 belongs to RDP, port 5900 belongs to VNC, and port 80 is standard unencrypted HTTP, none of which are SSH's default port.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1272$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$What is the default TCP port typically used by VNC (Virtual Network Computing)?$q$,$q$["5900","3389","22","21"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$VNC implementations typically default to TCP port 5900 for their remote screen-sharing sessions. Port 3389 belongs to RDP, port 22 belongs to SSH, and port 21 is the standard FTP control port - none of them are VNC's default.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1273$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$What does a VPN (Virtual Private Network) primarily provide when a remote employee connects to the office network over the internet?$q$,$q$["An encrypted tunnel between the remote device and the office network, protecting traffic in transit over the untrusted internet","A faster internet connection speed regardless of the underlying ISP","Automatic antivirus scanning of the remote device","A guarantee that the remote device cannot be lost or stolen"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$A VPN's core function is establishing an encrypted tunnel between the remote device and the private network, protecting the traffic that crosses the untrusted public internet from interception or tampering. It has no effect on the underlying connection speed provided by the ISP, doesn't scan for viruses, and can't prevent physical loss or theft of the device.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1274$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which of the following meaningfully reduce the risk of exposing Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) to attackers? (Select three.)$q$,$q$["Requiring a VPN connection before RDP is reachable at all, instead of exposing it directly to the internet","Enabling Network Level Authentication (NLA)","Requiring multifactor authentication for the remote access account","Leaving RDP's default port open directly to the internet for convenience"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Requiring a VPN before RDP is reachable at all keeps the RDP port off the open internet entirely, enabling NLA forces authentication before a full session is even established, and requiring MFA means a stolen password alone isn't enough - all three meaningfully reduce risk. Leaving RDP's default port open directly to the internet is the opposite of a mitigation; it's the exposure these other measures are meant to guard against.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1275$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A technician needs to remotely help an end user troubleshoot an issue, with the user watching the session and able to see or take back control at any time. Which type of tool is designed for this rather than for unattended full remote control?$q$,$q$["Remote assistance/screen-sharing software, such as Windows Quick Assist, that requires the end user's active participation and visible consent","An RDP session configured for unattended access with no user present","SSH connecting to a headless server with no display","A VPN client with no remote-control feature at all"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Remote assistance tools such as Windows Quick Assist are specifically designed around the end user granting access and watching (or even taking back control during) the session, unlike unattended access tools meant to connect without anyone present. An unattended RDP session, a headless SSH connection with no display, and a VPN client alone don't provide that kind of interactive, user-supervised screen-sharing experience.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1276$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Why is SSH preferred over the older Telnet protocol for remote command-line access?$q$,$q$["SSH encrypts the entire session, including login credentials, while Telnet transmits everything, including passwords, in plain text","SSH only works on Windows, while Telnet works on every operating system","Telnet requires a graphical desktop session, while SSH does not","SSH cannot be used to run any commands, only to view a session"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$SSH encrypts the entire session end to end, including the login credentials, while Telnet transmits everything - commands and passwords alike - in plain text that can be captured by anyone observing the network traffic. SSH is available across virtually every major OS, not Windows-only, and SSH is fully capable of running commands, not just viewing a passive session.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1277$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$ordering$q$,2,$q$Put these steps for securely enabling remote access to a workstation into the correct order.$q$,$q$["Configure the firewall to allow the remote-access port only from the specific trusted source IP address(es) or VPN range","Require connections to arrive through a VPN rather than being exposed directly to the internet","Enable Network Level Authentication for the remote-access protocol","Turn on the remote-access feature (such as RDP) on the workstation itself"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[3,2,1,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$Turning on the remote-access feature itself comes first, then hardening its authentication with NLA, then requiring connections to arrive only through a VPN, and finally restricting the firewall to allow that port only from the trusted VPN range or specific source IPs - layering protections from the feature outward to the network boundary.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1278$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$matching$q$,2,$q$Match each remote access technology to its default port and typical use case.$q$,$q${"left":["RDP","SSH","VNC","HTTPS-based VPN portal"],"right":["TCP 443 - used by many SSL/TLS VPN client portals and secure web-based remote access gateways","TCP 3389 - Windows graphical remote desktop control","TCP 22 - encrypted remote command-line login and file transfer","TCP 5900 - cross-platform remote screen-sharing"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,2,3,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$RDP defaults to TCP 3389 for Windows graphical remote desktop control, SSH defaults to TCP 22 for encrypted command-line access, VNC defaults to TCP 5900 for cross-platform screen-sharing, and an HTTPS-based VPN portal typically runs over TCP 443, the standard port used by many SSL/TLS VPN client gateways.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1279$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Why is multifactor authentication (MFA) especially important for accounts used to access systems remotely, such as through VPN or RDP?$q$,$q$["It ensures that a stolen or guessed password alone is not enough for an attacker to gain remote access, requiring an additional factor they are unlikely to also possess","It replaces the need for a username entirely","It automatically encrypts all files on the remote system","It permanently disables remote access after a single failed login"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$MFA ensures that knowing, guessing, or stealing a password by itself isn't enough to log in remotely, since the attacker would also need to produce the additional factor - something the legitimate user has or is. It doesn't remove the need for a username, doesn't encrypt files on the target system, and doesn't lock out remote access after one failed attempt.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1280$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A technician learns that a particular VNC implementation in use provides weak or no encryption by default for its remote desktop sessions. What is the recommended way to secure this connection when access is needed over the internet?$q$,$q$["Tunnel the VNC connection through SSH or a VPN so the traffic is encrypted in transit","Continue using it as-is, since VNC traffic cannot be intercepted","Switch the VNC port from 5900 to 3389 to gain RDP's encryption automatically","Disable the firewall so VNC connects more reliably"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Tunneling the VNC session through SSH or a VPN wraps the otherwise weakly (or un-) encrypted VNC traffic in a strong, encrypted channel for its trip across the internet, without requiring the VNC software itself to support strong encryption. VNC traffic absolutely can be intercepted if unencrypted, changing the port number to 3389 does not turn VNC into RDP or add encryption, and disabling the firewall only increases exposure.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1281$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$multi$q$,3,$q$Which of the following correctly describe the difference between split-tunnel and full-tunnel VPN configurations? (Select two.)$q$,$q$["In a full tunnel, all of the remote device's internet traffic is routed through the VPN, including traffic to sites unrelated to the office network","In a split tunnel, only traffic destined for the office/corporate network is routed through the VPN, while other internet traffic goes directly out through the local connection","A split tunnel configuration always provides stronger security than a full tunnel","Split tunneling and full tunneling are two names for the exact same VPN behavior"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$A full tunnel routes all of the remote device's internet traffic through the VPN, including traffic unrelated to the office network, while a split tunnel sends only office-bound traffic through the VPN and lets other traffic go directly out the local connection. Neither approach is universally "more secure" - each has tradeoffs - and they describe genuinely different, not identical, VPN behaviors.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1282$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$What additional risk does an RDP session without Network Level Authentication (NLA) enabled carry, compared to one with NLA enabled?$q$,$q$["A connection and a portion of the authentication process can begin before the user actually supplies valid credentials, exposing the session to certain pre-authentication attacks that NLA blocks by requiring authentication first","It becomes impossible for the session to be encrypted at all","The remote desktop resolution is permanently reduced","File transfer between the client and host stops working entirely"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Without NLA, a connection can begin, and part of the negotiation can occur, before the user actually supplies valid credentials, which exposes the session to certain pre-authentication attacks that NLA closes off by requiring authentication up front before the full desktop session is created. It has no effect on whether encryption is possible at all, doesn't reduce screen resolution, and doesn't disable file transfer.$q$),
($q$a2-q-1283$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$What key difference distinguishes remote assistance software from unattended remote control/access software?$q$,$q$["Remote assistance typically requires the end user present at the remote machine to grant and visibly monitor the session, while unattended remote access can connect without anyone present at that end","Remote assistance software cannot transmit any screen image at all","Unattended remote access requires a physical keyboard connected at the remote machine at all times","There is no meaningful difference between the two categories"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Remote assistance software is built around requiring the end user present at the remote machine to grant and typically watch the session, whereas unattended remote access is set up in advance to allow connecting in without anyone present at that end. Remote assistance tools do transmit the screen, unattended access does not require a physically connected keyboard at all times, and there is a real, meaningful distinction between the two categories.$q$);

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 1.10 macOS (a2-f-1200..a2-f-1216)
($q$a2-f-1200$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$What does Quick Look (spacebar) let you do in Finder?$q$,$q$Preview a selected file's contents (documents, images, PDFs) without opening the file in its full application.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1201$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$What is a Smart Folder in Finder?$q$,$q$A folder that automatically displays files matching saved search criteria, updating live as matching files are added or removed, rather than storing files itself.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1202$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$What information does Get Info (Command+I) show about a file in Finder?$q$,$q$Size, kind, where it's located, sharing & permissions, tags, and which application opens it by default.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1203$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$How can Spotlight searches be narrowed beyond a plain keyword?$q$,$q$With search operators such as kind:pdf or from:, and date-based modifiers, which filter results by file type, sender, or date rather than plain keyword text alone.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1204$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$What does Mission Control show on macOS?$q$,$q$An overview of all open windows, Spaces (virtual desktops), and full-screen apps, letting a user quickly switch between them.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1205$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$What is the keyboard shortcut to force quit a frozen application, and where else can Force Quit be accessed?$q$,$q$Command+Option+Escape opens the Force Quit Applications window; it's also reachable from the Apple menu.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1206$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$What is Activity Monitor used for on macOS?$q$,$q$Viewing running processes and their CPU/memory/energy/disk usage, and force-quitting a hung or resource-heavy process, similar to Windows Task Manager.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1207$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$What does the man command do in Terminal?$q$,$q$Displays the manual page (usage, options, examples) for a given command.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1208$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$What is the difference between ls and ls -la in Terminal?$q$,$q$ls lists visible files/folders in the current directory; ls -la adds hidden (dot) files and shows a long-format listing with permissions, owner, size, and modification date.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1209$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$Why is sudo required before some Terminal commands on macOS?$q$,$q$Those commands modify system-protected files/locations, so sudo temporarily elevates the command to run with administrator privileges after the user authenticates.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1210$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$What does Disk Utility's Partition function let you do?$q$,$q$Divide a single physical drive into multiple logical volumes, each with its own file system and size, without needing separate physical disks.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1211$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$What is the difference between Erase and First Aid in Disk Utility?$q$,$q$Erase reformats a drive/volume, wiping its data and applying a chosen file system; First Aid checks a drive/volume for file-system errors and attempts non-destructive repairs.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1212$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$What does brew update do, versus brew upgrade?$q$,$q$brew update refreshes Homebrew's local list of available formula/cask versions; brew upgrade actually installs newer versions of packages already installed on the system.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1213$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$APFS$q$,$q$Apple File System - the default modern file system for Mac startup drives since macOS 10.13, optimized for SSD/flash storage with native encryption, snapshots, and space sharing across volumes.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1214$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$HFS+$q$,$q$Hierarchical File System Plus (Mac OS Extended) - the older Apple file system predating APFS, still offered in Disk Utility for compatibility with older drives, backups, or devices.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1215$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$Explain why Time Machine can restore a file to a much earlier version even though the source file on the Mac has since been overwritten.$q$,$q$Model answer: Time Machine keeps incremental, versioned snapshots rather than a single mirrored copy; each backup after the first stores only what changed, but every earlier snapshot remains browsable, so a user can step back in time to a snapshot taken before the overwrite and restore that exact earlier version, independent of the file's current state on the Mac.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1216$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.10$q$,$q$Explain why Homebrew commands should not routinely be run with sudo.$q$,$q$Model answer: Homebrew installs and manages its formulas within directories already owned by the current user (historically /usr/local, or /opt/homebrew on Apple Silicon), so it does not need elevated privileges for normal operation. Running brew with sudo can create files owned by root inside that user-owned tree, which then blocks Homebrew's own user-level processes from writing to or updating those files later, causing permission errors on subsequent installs/upgrades.$q$),
-- 1.11 Linux (a2-f-1217..a2-f-1230)
($q$a2-f-1217$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$What does the ls command do in a Linux/Unix shell?$q$,$q$Lists the files and subdirectories contained in the current (or a specified) directory.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1218$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$What is the difference between cp and mv in Linux?$q$,$q$cp copies a file or directory to a new location, leaving the original in place; mv moves (or renames) it, removing it from the original location.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1219$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$Why is the rm -rf command considered especially dangerous?$q$,$q$It recursively (-r) and forcibly (-f) deletes files and directories without confirmation prompts or a way to undo, so a mistaken path - especially with wildcards or as root - can permanently wipe far more than intended.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1220$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$What does the grep command search, and what does it return?$q$,$q$It searches the given file(s) or input for lines matching a specified pattern (plain text or regular expression) and prints the matching lines.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1221$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$What is the difference between the ps and top commands?$q$,$q$ps prints a one-time snapshot of running processes; top displays a continuously refreshing, interactive view of processes ranked by resource usage.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1222$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$What does the kill command do, and what is kill -9 specifically?$q$,$q$kill sends a signal (by default, a polite request to terminate) to a running process by its PID; kill -9 sends SIGKILL, forcibly terminating the process immediately without letting it clean up.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1223$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$What is the purpose of the /etc directory?$q$,$q$It holds essential system-wide and application configuration files, such as network settings and user/group definitions.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1224$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$What is the purpose of the /var/log directory?$q$,$q$It stores log files generated by the operating system and installed services, commonly reviewed first when troubleshooting a failure.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1225$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$What is the key functional difference between apt and yum/dnf?$q$,$q$Both are command-line package managers that install, update, and remove software while resolving dependencies; apt is used on Debian-family distributions (Ubuntu, Debian), while yum/dnf are used on Red Hat-family distributions (RHEL, CentOS, Fedora).$q$),
($q$a2-f-1226$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$What does chmod u+x filename do?$q$,$q$It adds execute permission for the file's owner (user) only, leaving group and other permissions unchanged, using symbolic rather than numeric syntax.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1227$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$APT$q$,$q$Advanced Package Tool - the command-line package management system used by Debian-based Linux distributions (including Ubuntu) to install, update, and remove software and resolve dependencies.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1228$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$FHS$q$,$q$Filesystem Hierarchy Standard - the specification defining the standard purpose of top-level Linux directories such as /etc, /home, /var, and /bin, so software and administrators can rely on consistent file locations across distributions.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1229$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$Explain why running a single command with sudo is generally safer than logging into a full root session.$q$,$q$Model answer: sudo elevates only the one command that was explicitly typed, requires the invoking user's own password rather than a separate shared root password, and logs that action against the real user's identity for accountability. A full root login session removes those per-command checkpoints - every subsequent command in that session runs with unrestricted privileges and no per-action confirmation, so one mistake, such as a stray rm with the wrong path, executes immediately with full system access.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1230$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.11$q$,$q$Explain the difference between chmod's numeric syntax (e.g., chmod 750 file) and its symbolic syntax (e.g., chmod u+rwx,g+rx file), and why both express the same idea.$q$,$q$Model answer: Numeric mode encodes permissions as a three-digit octal number where each digit sums read(4)+write(2)+execute(1) for owner, group, and other respectively (750 = owner rwx, group r-x, other none). Symbolic mode instead lists targets (u/g/o/a) and adds/removes/sets specific permission letters (r, w, x) directly. Both change the same underlying permission bits; numeric mode sets the full permission set in one step, while symbolic mode is often easier for making a small, incremental change without needing to recompute the whole octal value.$q$),
-- 2.9 SOHO networking (a2-f-1231..a2-f-1247)
($q$a2-f-1231$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$Why should the default administrator password be changed on a new SOHO router?$q$,$q$Default admin credentials for most router models are publicly documented, so leaving them unchanged lets anyone on the network (or in some cases the internet) log in and reconfigure or compromise the router.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1232$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$Why are Wi-Fi channels 1, 6, and 11 recommended for 2.4 GHz networks?$q$,$q$They are the only three 20 MHz-wide channels in the 2.4 GHz band that don't overlap with each other, minimizing interference between nearby networks compared to using arbitrary adjacent channels.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1233$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$What is the general range/throughput tradeoff between 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz Wi-Fi?$q$,$q$2.4 GHz travels farther and penetrates walls better but is slower and more congested; 5 GHz offers higher throughput and less interference but has shorter range and weaker wall penetration.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1234$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$What does port forwarding do on a SOHO router?$q$,$q$It maps a specific external (WAN-side) port to an internal device's IP address and port, so inbound internet traffic on that port reaches the intended internal device instead of being blocked by NAT/the firewall.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1235$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$What is a DMZ host on a SOHO router, and why is it riskier than port forwarding?$q$,$q$A DMZ host exposes one internal device to essentially all unsolicited inbound traffic, bypassing normal firewall filtering entirely, whereas port forwarding only opens the specific port(s) actually needed.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1236$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$Why should WAN-side (remote) administrative access typically be disabled on a home router?$q$,$q$Leaving it enabled exposes the router's login and configuration page directly to the internet, making it a target for automated scanning and credential-stuffing attacks; management should normally be done only from inside the LAN.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1237$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$Why is a DHCP reservation useful for a device that has port forwarding configured to it?$q$,$q$It guarantees that device always receives the same internal IP address from the DHCP server, so the port-forwarding rule, which points at a specific IP, keeps working even after the device reboots or its lease renews.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1238$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$What is the purpose of a guest network on a SOHO router?$q$,$q$It gives visitors and untrusted devices internet access while keeping them on a separate logical network, isolated from the main LAN's computers, shared folders, and printers.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1239$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$Why should a router's firmware be kept up to date?$q$,$q$Firmware updates patch known security vulnerabilities and bugs; an out-of-date router can remain exploitable even after every other setting has been hardened.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1240$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$What does disabling SSID broadcast actually accomplish, and what doesn't it accomplish?$q$,$q$It hides the network name from casual browsing/nearby device lists, but it does not meaningfully stop a motivated attacker, since the SSID is still visible in the wireless traffic itself to anyone using basic monitoring tools - it is not a substitute for strong encryption.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1241$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$What should be verified first when a service that was reachable internally suddenly cannot be reached from the internet, even though port forwarding looks correctly configured?$q$,$q$Whether an inbound firewall rule is still blocking that port, and whether the internal device's own IP address changed, which would break the forwarding rule's target.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1242$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$SSID$q$,$q$Service Set Identifier - the (usually human-readable) name broadcast by a wireless access point that identifies a specific Wi-Fi network to devices choosing which network to join.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1243$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$SOHO$q$,$q$Small Office/Home Office - a network environment with only one router/access point serving a small number of users and devices, typically without dedicated IT staff or enterprise-grade equipment.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1244$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$IoT$q$,$q$Internet of Things - network-connected everyday devices, such as thermostats, cameras, smart plugs, and doorbells, that often receive infrequent security updates and should be isolated on a separate network segment from sensitive devices.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1245$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$WPS$q$,$q$Wi-Fi Protected Setup - a feature meant to simplify joining a wireless network, often via a PIN or push-button, whose PIN mode has known brute-force weaknesses and is commonly recommended to be disabled.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1246$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$Explain why leaving a router's default administrator credentials unchanged is a serious risk even if the Wi-Fi password itself is strong.$q$,$q$Model answer: The Wi-Fi passphrase only controls who can join the wireless network, while the administrator credentials control the router's configuration itself. Default admin logins for most router models are published online and reused across thousands of units, so anyone who reaches the router's management interface - over Wi-Fi, a wired LAN port, or in some misconfigured cases the WAN - can log in with the known default and reconfigure DNS, port forwarding, or firmware regardless of how strong the separate Wi-Fi password is.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1247$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,2,$q$2.9$q$,$q$Explain why isolating IoT devices on their own network segment limits the impact of a single compromised device.$q$,$q$Model answer: Many consumer IoT devices run outdated, rarely patched firmware and have weak built-in security, making them attractive targets. If a compromised IoT device sits on the same network as computers and phones, an attacker who takes it over can pivot and probe those other devices directly. Placing IoT devices on a separate guest network or VLAN with client isolation means that even if one is compromised, it cannot reach or attack the devices holding sensitive data on the main network.$q$),
-- 2.10 Browser security (a2-f-1248..a2-f-1265)
($q$a2-f-1248$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$What does a browser's pop-up blocker prevent by default?$q$,$q$Unrequested windows that open automatically without the user clicking a link intended to open a new window, commonly used to deliver ads or scam pages.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1249$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$What does private/incognito browsing actually hide, and what does it not hide?$q$,$q$It stops the browser from saving local history, cookies, cache, and form data for that session; it does not hide the user's activity from the websites visited, the network operator, or the ISP.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1250$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$What is browser sandboxing, and why does it matter for security?$q$,$q$Running each tab, site, or plugin in its own isolated process so that if one is compromised or crashes, it cannot easily access the rest of the browser, the operating system, or other open tabs.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1251$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$What can cause a legitimate certificate warning to appear on a valid website?$q$,$q$An expired certificate, a certificate issued for a different domain name than the one being visited, or a certificate signed by an untrusted or self-signed authority.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1252$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$Why shouldn't a user simply click through a certificate warning to reach a login page?$q$,$q$The warning means the browser could not verify the site's identity or that encryption is properly set up, which is exactly the situation a man-in-the-middle attacker intercepting traffic to steal credentials would create.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1253$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$What does clearing a browser's cookies typically fix?$q$,$q$Login loops, sites showing stale or incorrect information, or being unexpectedly signed out, since it removes locally stored session and preference data that may have become corrupted or outdated.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1254$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$Why should extension permissions be reviewed before installing?$q$,$q$To confirm the extension isn't requesting far more access, such as reading or changing data on every website, than its stated purpose needs, limiting what damage a malicious or compromised extension could do.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1255$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$What is a common symptom of a malicious or adware-bundled browser extension?$q$,$q$Unexpected pop-up ads, the browser's homepage or default search engine changing without the user's action, or unexplained redirects to advertising pages.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1256$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$What is the difference between browser cache and cookies?$q$,$q$Cache stores downloaded page assets, such as images, scripts, and style sheets, locally to speed up future page loads; cookies store small pieces of site-specific data, such as login sessions and preferences.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1257$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$Why does a password manager's autofill behavior help resist certain phishing sites?$q$,$q$It ties saved credentials to the exact domain they were created for, so it generally will not autofill them on a similar-looking but different fraudulent domain, unlike a user who might type credentials in manually without noticing the mismatch.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1258$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$Why is keeping a browser updated important for security?$q$,$q$Browser updates patch newly discovered vulnerabilities in the rendering engine, JavaScript engine, and extension APIs that attackers could otherwise exploit through a malicious webpage.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1259$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$What should a technician check first when a user's browser is redirecting to unexpected ad pages?$q$,$q$The list of installed extensions/add-ons for anything unfamiliar, recently added, or with excessive permissions, since malicious or adware extensions are a common cause.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1260$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$Why is a plugin/extension from an official browser store generally safer than one sideloaded from an unofficial source?$q$,$q$Official stores apply at least baseline review and can quickly remove or disable extensions found to be malicious after the fact, while sideloaded extensions bypass that review entirely.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1261$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$What local browsing data survives closing a private/incognito window?$q$,$q$Files that were explicitly downloaded and saved to disk, and bookmarks manually added during the session - private mode only avoids saving history, cookies, cache, and form data.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1262$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$TLS$q$,$q$Transport Layer Security - the encryption protocol behind HTTPS that protects data in transit between a browser and a website; certificate warnings usually indicate a problem verifying the site's TLS certificate.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1263$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$CA$q$,$q$Certificate Authority - a trusted organization that issues and vouches for digital certificates; a browser's certificate warning often means it cannot verify the certificate was issued by a CA it trusts, or that it doesn't match the domain.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1264$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$Explain why a browser certificate warning should never simply be clicked through on a site where credentials or payment information will be entered.$q$,$q$Model answer: A certificate warning means the browser could not confirm that the site is who it claims to be, or that the connection is properly encrypted end-to-end. That is precisely the scenario an attacker performing a man-in-the-middle interception would create - presenting a self-signed or mismatched certificate while secretly relaying and reading traffic between the user and the real site. Clicking through removes the one built-in signal warning the user that the connection cannot be trusted, right before they would type in sensitive data.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1265$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,2,$q$2.10$q$,$q$Explain how a password manager reduces the practical impact of any single website suffering a data breach.$q$,$q$Model answer: A password manager makes it practical to use a long, random, unique password for every single account instead of reusing the same password everywhere. If one site is breached and its password database leaks, only that one site's unique password is exposed - it cannot be reused by attackers to log into the user's other accounts (a technique called credential stuffing), which is exactly the risk created when the same password is reused across many sites.$q$),
-- 4.8 Scripting basics (a2-f-1266..a2-f-1283)
($q$a2-f-1266$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$What is the syntax difference between a Windows batch variable reference and a Bash variable reference?$q$,$q$Batch wraps the variable name in percent signs, such as %USERNAME%, while Bash prefixes it with a dollar sign, such as $USERNAME or ${USERNAME}.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1267$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$How does PowerShell denote a variable, and how does that differ from Bash's assignment style?$q$,$q$PowerShell variables are always prefixed with a dollar sign, both when assigning and when referencing them, for example $name = "value", whereas Bash uses the dollar sign only when reading a variable's value, not when assigning it.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1268$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$Why is VBScript still occasionally encountered in older environments despite being deprecated?$q$,$q$Some legacy logon scripts, Office macros, and older automation tooling still rely on it, but Microsoft has phased out and restricted its execution by default because it has been widely abused as a malware delivery mechanism.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1269$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$What is a common IT use case for a Bash or Python script that loops through a list of hostnames?$q$,$q$Automatically testing connectivity, for example sending a ping to each one, or collecting status/inventory information across many machines without manually checking each one individually.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1270$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$What does a while loop do differently from a for loop?$q$,$q$A for loop typically repeats a fixed number of times or once per item in a known list; a while loop keeps repeating as long as a specified condition remains true, without necessarily knowing in advance how many iterations that will take.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1271$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$Why is hardcoding a plaintext password inside a script a serious risk?$q$,$q$Anyone who can read the script file - including through version control history, backups, or simple file access - can see the credential in plain text, unlike using a secure credential store, environment variable, or prompted input.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1272$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$What character starts a comment line in Bash, and what does REM do in a batch file?$q$,$q$Bash comments begin with #; REM marks a comment line in a Windows batch script - both are ignored when the script actually runs and exist purely to document the code for humans.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1273$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$What is the shebang line (e.g., #!/bin/bash) at the top of a script, and what does it do?$q$,$q$It tells the operating system which interpreter should be used to execute the rest of the script file when it's run directly, rather than requiring the user to specify the interpreter manually every time.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1274$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$Why should a script that will run unattended include error handling rather than assuming every command succeeds?$q$,$q$Without it, one failed step, such as a missing file or an unreachable network path, can cause the rest of the script to behave unpredictably, silently skip important work, or make changes based on incorrect assumptions about what already happened.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1275$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$What is a practical reason a technician would automate account creation with a script instead of doing it manually in a GUI?$q$,$q$It ensures every new account is created with the same consistent settings and permissions, saves time when onboarding many users at once, and reduces the chance of a manual step being missed.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1276$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$Why is testing a new script in a non-production/sandbox environment recommended before running it against live systems?$q$,$q$It lets a technician confirm the script does exactly what is intended, and catch mistakes such as an overly broad delete or an unintended change, without risking real user data or production services.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1277$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$What is the security concern with a script that requests more privileges than its task actually needs?$q$,$q$If the script has a bug or is later modified maliciously, it can then do far more damage than necessary, since it already holds broader access than the task required - violating the principle of least privilege.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1278$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$What does it mean that Python is an interpreted, indentation-sensitive language?$q$,$q$Its code runs line by line through the Python interpreter without a separate compile-to-binary step, and consistent indentation, rather than braces or keywords, is what actually defines which lines belong inside a loop, function, or conditional block.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1279$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$Why might VBScript or batch macros embedded in an email attachment be flagged as a security risk?$q$,$q$They can execute automatically or with a single click, running arbitrary commands, such as downloading further malware or modifying the registry, with the privileges of the user who opened the attachment, making them a common phishing delivery method.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1280$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$What is the practical difference between running a script manually and scheduling it, for example with Task Scheduler or cron?$q$,$q$A manually run script executes only when someone starts it; a scheduled script runs automatically at defined times or intervals without requiring a person to remember or trigger it each time.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1281$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$ISE$q$,$q$Integrated Scripting Environment - the built-in PowerShell editor (Windows PowerShell ISE) that provides script editing, a console pane, and basic debugging in one window, though it has been superseded on newer systems by tools like Visual Studio Code.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1282$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$Explain why a script should never contain a hardcoded, plaintext administrative password.$q$,$q$Model answer: A script file is often stored, copied, emailed, or checked into version control alongside other project files, and anyone with read access to it - now or in its history - can see a plaintext password in full. If that password is reused anywhere else or grants broad administrative access, its exposure compromises far more than just the one script's function. Best practice is to prompt for the credential at run time, pull it from a secured credential store or vault, or use a scoped service account/token instead of embedding a real password directly in the file.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1283$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,4,$q$4.8$q$,$q$Explain why it matters to test a new automation script in a sandbox or non-production environment before running it on live systems.$q$,$q$Model answer: Scripts that touch files, accounts, or configuration can have unintended side effects from a small logic error - a loop that runs one iteration too many, a delete command matching more files than intended, or a wrong variable value. Running it first in an isolated test environment lets a technician observe and fix those mistakes safely, without risking real user data, live services, or requiring a disruptive rollback, before trusting the script against production systems.$q$),
-- 4.9 Remote access technologies (a2-f-1284..a2-f-1302)
($q$a2-f-1284$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$What is the default TCP port for RDP, and what does it carry?$q$,$q$Port 3389; it carries the encrypted graphical remote desktop session used to control a Windows PC's full desktop remotely.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1285$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$What is the default TCP port for SSH, and what does it carry?$q$,$q$Port 22; it carries an encrypted remote command-line session and can also tunnel file transfers such as SCP/SFTP.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1286$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$What is the default TCP port for VNC, and what does it carry?$q$,$q$Port 5900; it carries a remote desktop screen-sharing session, typically with weaker or no built-in encryption compared to RDP or SSH, depending on the implementation.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1287$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$What does a VPN protect when a remote worker connects to the office network over the internet?$q$,$q$It creates an encrypted tunnel for traffic between the remote device and the office network, protecting it from interception while crossing the untrusted public internet.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1288$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$What is the difference between a split-tunnel and a full-tunnel VPN?$q$,$q$A split tunnel sends only office/corporate-bound traffic through the VPN while other internet traffic goes out directly; a full tunnel routes all of the device's internet traffic through the VPN.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1289$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$What does Network Level Authentication (NLA) add to RDP security?$q$,$q$It requires the connecting user to authenticate before a full remote desktop session is established, reducing exposure to certain pre-authentication attacks compared to authenticating only after the session begins.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1290$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$Why does multifactor authentication matter specifically for remote access accounts such as VPN and RDP?$q$,$q$Remote access accounts are reachable from anywhere with credentials, so MFA ensures a stolen or guessed password alone is not enough for an attacker to get in - they'd also need the second factor.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1291$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$What is the difference between remote assistance software, like Quick Assist, and unattended remote access software?$q$,$q$Remote assistance requires someone present at the remote machine to grant access and typically watch or participate in the session; unattended remote access is pre-configured to allow a connection without anyone present at that end.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1292$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$Why should RDP or SSH generally not be exposed directly to the open internet without additional protection?$q$,$q$Directly exposed management ports are constantly scanned and targeted by automated brute-force and exploit attempts; requiring a VPN first, or restricting access by source IP, keeps the service reachable only to already-trusted connections.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1293$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$Why is Telnet considered insecure compared to SSH for remote command-line access?$q$,$q$Telnet sends the entire session, including login credentials, in plain text, so anyone able to observe the network traffic can capture the password; SSH encrypts the whole session.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1294$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$What is a firewall rule restricting RDP/SSH access by source IP address meant to accomplish?$q$,$q$It limits inbound connection attempts on that port to only the specific trusted address(es), such as a VPN's IP range or a known office location, rather than accepting attempts from anywhere on the internet.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1295$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$Why does an unencrypted or weakly encrypted VNC session pose a risk over the internet?$q$,$q$Screen contents, keystrokes, and credentials typed during the session could be intercepted in transit; tunneling the VNC connection through SSH or a VPN adds the encryption the VNC implementation itself lacks.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1296$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$What is SSH key-based authentication, and why is it generally considered stronger than password authentication?$q$,$q$The client presents a cryptographic private key matching a public key already authorized on the server, rather than typing a password; it resists brute-force guessing and password-reuse/phishing risks since there's no password to steal or guess in the first place.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1297$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$What is a practical security tradeoff of a full-tunnel VPN compared to a split tunnel?$q$,$q$A full tunnel routes all traffic, including non-work browsing, through the corporate network, which can be inspected and filtered there for security but also adds load to that connection and slows unrelated browsing; a split tunnel reduces that load but leaves non-corporate traffic unprotected by the VPN and uninspected by the corporate network.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1298$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$RDP$q$,$q$Remote Desktop Protocol - Microsoft's protocol for remotely controlling a Windows PC's full graphical desktop, using TCP port 3389 by default and requiring Windows Pro/Enterprise to host inbound sessions.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1299$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$VPN$q$,$q$Virtual Private Network - creates an encrypted tunnel for traffic between a remote device and a private network over an untrusted network such as the internet, protecting data in transit and often extending network-level access to internal resources.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1300$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$NLA$q$,$q$Network Level Authentication - an RDP security feature that requires a user to authenticate before a full remote desktop session is established, reducing exposure to pre-authentication attacks compared to RDP without it.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1301$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$Explain why RDP over the open internet without a VPN is risky.$q$,$q$Model answer: RDP exposed directly to the internet on its default port is continuously scanned by automated tools looking for exactly that service, then hit with brute-force login attempts or checked against known RDP vulnerabilities. Because a successful compromise hands the attacker a full interactive graphical desktop session, this is a high-value, high-visibility target. Requiring a VPN connection first means the RDP port is never reachable from the open internet at all - only from devices that have already authenticated onto the private network - removing it from the pool of directly attackable internet-facing services, and adding NLA and MFA on top gives further layers of defense.$q$),
($q$a2-f-1302$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,4,$q$4.9$q$,$q$Explain why VNC's traffic should typically be tunneled through SSH or a VPN when used over the internet.$q$,$q$Model answer: Many VNC implementations either use weak encryption or, in some configurations, none at all, transmitting the screen image, keystrokes, and sometimes even the connection password in a form that could be captured by anyone able to observe the network traffic in between. Tunneling the VNC session inside an already-encrypted SSH connection or a VPN wraps that otherwise-exposed traffic in strong encryption for the portion of its path that crosses an untrusted network, without requiring the VNC software itself to support strong encryption natively.$q$);
